Compare each pair of experimental relevance buckets for its relative usefulness
in predicting whether a molecule causes immune-mediated skin sensitization or
contact allergy under the reported condition.

This target is narrower than general skin reaction. Irritation, corrosion,
phototoxicity, photoallergy, urticaria, and generic local tissue injury are not
the target. Mechanistic sensitization-AOP and skin-exposure measurements may be
useful evidence, but are not automatically direct sensitization outcomes.

Every comparison is within one source. Each comparison contains both candidates
inline. Choose the exact `bucket_id` of the candidate whose measurement type
would reduce uncertainty about the target more. Candidate order has no meaning.

Rules:

1. Judge how informative the kind of measurement would be, regardless of
   whether its observed result was positive or negative. Sample cards omit
   result values, labels, counts, and directions.
2. Hold chemical similarity, assay-transfer reliability, evidence quality, and
   evidence quantity equal.
3. Compare the information supplied by the endpoint, assay or study design, and
   biological system represented by each candidate.
4. A direct-source bucket is not automatically useful: its endpoint must
   genuinely constrain sensitization or contact allergy.
5. Irritation, phototoxicity, and exposure evidence can win within their source
   when one candidate is more informative about sensitization risk than another.
6. Sample records only clarify the measurement and experimental setting. Do not
   reward sample count, repetition, or prose detail.
7. Make a strict binary choice. Select the more useful candidate even when the
   advantage is small; ties are not allowed.
8. Evaluate every comparison independently. Never use `candidate_1`,
   `candidate_2`, A, or B as the winner.

